WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.080
Consumption Central helps you understand Copilot credits and AI spend.

2
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:12.615
This is a practical guide to every current app view: what it shows, how to read it, and what action to take.

3
00:00:12.615 --> 00:00:16.106
We use genuine app screens with synthetic sample data.

4
00:00:16.106 --> 00:00:19.210
The people, agents, organisations and amounts are fictional.

5
00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.150
The voice is AI generated.

6
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:29.449
The six main tabs are Overview, Cowork and Work IQ, Copilot Studio, GitHub Copilot, Azure AI Foundry, and Guide.

7
00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:34.237
They answer related questions, but they do not measure the same thing.

8
00:00:34.237 --> 00:00:39.026
A credit in one product is not a universal unit of work.

9
00:00:39.026 --> 00:00:43.017
More credits do not, on their own, prove more value.

10
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.188
Work through the app in that order.

11
00:00:46.188 --> 00:00:49.743
Check which source is loaded and which dates it covers.

12
00:00:49.743 --> 00:00:51.165
Find the largest driver.

13
00:00:51.165 --> 00:00:55.075
Then use the relevant detail page to decide what to investigate.

14
00:00:55.075 --> 00:01:03.250
This is a guide to reading the app, not an installer walkthrough, a live service demonstration, or a claim about current commercial prices.

15
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:11.225
The date range in the header describes the loaded dataset, not a single period applied to every page.

16
00:01:11.225 --> 00:01:13.650
Overview has From and To dates.

17
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.267
Cowork has weekly periods.

18
00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:17.288
Studio has a tenant period.

19
00:01:17.288 --> 00:01:18.904
GitHub has monthly periods.

20
00:01:18.904 --> 00:01:24.158
Azure spend uses the loaded export, while reconciliation has its own billing-period selector.

21
00:01:24.158 --> 00:01:28.200
Always read the controls on the page you are using.

22
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:33.601
Within a product, move between its views to keep investigating the selection.

23
00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:36.363
Changing the main tab clears the filters.

24
00:01:36.363 --> 00:01:41.887
Group by controls organise attributed people; they do not manufacture a missing identity join.

25
00:01:41.887 --> 00:01:44.254
Tables can be searched and sorted.

26
00:01:44.254 --> 00:01:48.594
Expand a group where offered to see its people or policies.

27
00:01:48.594 --> 00:01:50.567
Hover charts for additional measures.

28
00:01:51.283 --> 00:01:56.267
A missing source, an undefined ratio and no matching rows are different conditions.

29
00:01:56.267 --> 00:02:01.250
Read the notices and coverage before interpreting a dash or an empty chart.

30
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:05.850
A low count can be a short export rather than low demand.

31
00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:11.983
When comparing two views, first confirm they use the same time window, population and measurement basis.

32
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.277
Rates in the header opens the commercial assumptions.

33
00:02:16.277 --> 00:02:21.195
Cowork has prepaid and pay-as-you-go prices plus a capacity pack balance.

34
00:02:21.195 --> 00:02:24.772
Studio has its own prepaid and pay-as-you-go prices.

35
00:02:24.772 --> 00:02:31.479
GitHub has seat prices, a credit rate, future included credits and an allowance change date.

36
00:02:31.479 --> 00:02:35.950
These settings are editable planning inputs, not verified contract rates.

37
00:02:36.617 --> 00:02:42.401
If you change a credit price, the same recorded activity can produce a different cost estimate.

38
00:02:42.401 --> 00:02:44.571
It does not mean usage changed.

39
00:02:44.571 --> 00:02:49.271
The GitHub allowance date affects forecast months ending on or after that date.

40
00:02:49.271 --> 00:02:55.417
Check these settings against your agreement before you use an estimate for a purchasing or budgeting decision.

41
00:02:56.117 --> 00:03:02.832
The panel explains that changes take effect straight away and are not saved when you close the app.

42
00:03:02.832 --> 00:03:05.444
Reset to defaults restores the shipped assumptions.

43
00:03:05.444 --> 00:03:08.428
In this guide, all examples use synthetic data.

44
00:03:08.428 --> 00:03:15.517
Where we work through arithmetic, the example is explicitly fictional and is not being presented as a dashboard result.

45
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.405
Change data shows the loaded sources and lets you choose your own CSV exports.

46
00:03:21.405 --> 00:03:24.380
The app recognises sources from their column headers.

47
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.726
You do not need all four products to begin.

48
00:03:27.726 --> 00:03:36.650
In this recording we stay with the built-in synthetic sample; we do not connect to a tenant, load customer files or simulate live telemetry.

49
00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:45.308
Weekly credits, task categories, spending policies, Studio snapshots, GitHub billing usage and Azure exports each have different roles.

50
00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:49.720
Organisation data enriches grouping; it does not create consumption records.

51
00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.131
Check the source guide if a file is not recognised.

52
00:03:54.131 --> 00:03:59.867
Missing optional sources should remain missing, rather than being filled with invented usage.

53
00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:04.459
The app reads and calculates the selected files in your browser.

54
00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:11.240
The explainer link opens a separate public website only when you choose it; it does not upload those files.

55
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:15.523
In your own analysis, first confirm loaded row counts and date coverage.

56
00:04:15.523 --> 00:04:21.233
Do not mix an old identity export with a new billing period without checking the join.

57
00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:26.367
Overview places the four products on one selected date range.

58
00:04:26.367 --> 00:04:31.225
The cost bars rank them, but deliberately have no grand total.

59
00:04:31.225 --> 00:04:35.200
Cowork is a pay-as-you-go reference value before prepaid capacity.

60
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.292
Studio prices tenant quantities at configured rates.

61
00:04:38.292 --> 00:04:42.708
GitHub uses the export net usage amount without seat subscriptions.

62
00:04:42.708 --> 00:04:45.800
Azure uses the Foundry Models export cost.

63
00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:50.390
The credits table does not add a universal credit total either.

64
00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.614
Read the cost basis and coverage beside each row.

65
00:04:53.614 --> 00:04:57.554
Cowork counts whole weekly records whose dates fall in the range.

66
00:04:57.554 --> 00:05:01.852
Studio user and agent snapshots have no dates and are excluded here.

67
00:05:01.852 --> 00:05:06.150
That is why an Overview amount can differ from its product tab.

68
00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:12.067
Use Overview to choose where to investigate next, not to reconcile an invoice.

69
00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:14.067
Partial coverage is stated explicitly.

70
00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:17.667
No rows in selected dates is not confirmed zero.

71
00:05:17.667 --> 00:05:21.667
The Studio note also warns about credits without named-person attribution.

72
00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:28.467
Missing attribution is a reason to check coverage, not to allocate the gap to an arbitrary department.

73
00:05:29.117 --> 00:05:35.145
Cowork Consumption shows credits in the selected weekly window, the roster allowance and consuming users.

74
00:05:35.145 --> 00:05:38.762
The allowance is the sum of monthly person limits.

75
00:05:38.762 --> 00:05:45.192
If you choose eight weeks or all history, that denominator does not become an eight-week allowance.

76
00:05:45.192 --> 00:05:50.417
Read the period before concluding that someone has exceeded a like-for-like monthly budget.

77
00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:54.232
The group table shows where credits are concentrated.

78
00:05:54.232 --> 00:05:56.959
Expand a group to inspect its people.

79
00:05:56.959 --> 00:06:02.801
The line chart shows weekly credits, with weekly active users and week-on-week change on hover.

80
00:06:02.801 --> 00:06:13.317
The headline labelled Weekly active users is a distinct count across the weekly records, not an average of weekly counts and not necessarily the selected-period consuming count.

81
00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:18.484
Select a service, spending policy or cohort to narrow the investigation.

82
00:06:18.484 --> 00:06:22.126
Compare the pattern with rollout dates and expected work.

83
00:06:22.126 --> 00:06:26.577
A rise shared across many people differs from one high-consuming person.

84
00:06:26.577 --> 00:06:29.005
Use cost and task detail next.

85
00:06:29.005 --> 00:06:35.883
High consumption alone is neither a success measure nor sufficient evidence that a person should lose access.

86
00:06:36.533 --> 00:06:40.914
Cowork Cost prices credits using the capacity pack balance you enter.

87
00:06:40.914 --> 00:06:44.897
Prepaid used is the smaller of usage and that balance.

88
00:06:44.897 --> 00:06:47.287
Anything beyond the balance is pay-as-you-go.

89
00:06:47.287 --> 00:06:50.075
Total cost adds those two priced quantities.

90
00:06:50.075 --> 00:06:54.057
The blended rate is that total divided by credits used.

91
00:06:54.057 --> 00:06:59.633
This is a usage cost model, not the purchase price of an entire pack.

92
00:07:00.283 --> 00:07:04.672
Here is fictional arithmetic, not a dashboard result or contract price.

93
00:07:04.672 --> 00:07:19.433
If usage is ten thousand credits, the balance is six thousand, prepaid is eight tenths of a cent and pay-as-you-go is one cent, the model gives forty-eight dollars prepaid plus forty dollars pay-as-you-go: eighty-eight dollars in total.

94
00:07:20.117 --> 00:07:24.762
The weekly cost chart burns down the balance oldest week first.

95
00:07:24.762 --> 00:07:31.097
Policy allowance and headroom come from spending-policy plan limits, not the sum of person allowances.

96
00:07:31.097 --> 00:07:34.053
Missing policy data leaves that context unavailable.

97
00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:37.010
Use this view to test pricing assumptions.

98
00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:43.767
Do not sum independently priced group rows as a tenant allocation; use Chargeback for that job.

99
00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:51.013
Chargeback allocates Cowork cost by cost centre or department using optional fields on spending-policy metadata.

100
00:07:51.013 --> 00:07:56.263
The table carries billing owner and charge code and expands to policies.

101
00:07:56.263 --> 00:08:02.388
These are policy billing assignments, not necessarily the same grouping as a person department.

102
00:08:02.388 --> 00:08:07.200
Not assigned keeps unmapped cost visible rather than silently dropping it.

103
00:08:07.867 --> 00:08:14.682
The tenant prepaid share is prepaid credits divided by tenant credits for the selected period and service.

104
00:08:14.682 --> 00:08:20.696
Every line receives that same share at the prepaid rate and the remainder at pay-as-you-go.

105
00:08:20.696 --> 00:08:25.908
Filtering the visible groups does not recalculate a preferential rate for those groups.

106
00:08:25.908 --> 00:08:29.517
With no filters, chargeback matches the tenant Cowork cost.

107
00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:39.713
For a fictional sixty percent prepaid share, a line with a thousand credits receives six hundred prepaid and four hundred pay-as-you-go credits.

108
00:08:39.713 --> 00:08:46.631
Check its owner and policy, resolve Not assigned, then use Download CSV for the allocation detail.

109
00:08:46.631 --> 00:08:53.550
Fresh Viva spending-policy exports do not include the added billing columns; restore them after every re-export.

110
00:08:54.200 --> 00:09:00.185
Tasks adds a different lens: reported task counts, category mix and credit intensity.

111
00:09:00.185 --> 00:09:05.249
It uses its own task period, type, category and organisation filters.

112
00:09:05.249 --> 00:09:08.472
The stacked weekly chart shows task types.

113
00:09:08.472 --> 00:09:12.615
Credits per task divides attributed credits by reported tasks.

114
00:09:12.615 --> 00:09:18.600
Those tasks are observed export counts, not a measured count of successful outcomes.

115
00:09:19.283 --> 00:09:25.371
Expert-equivalent hours multiply task counts by provisional minutes per category, then divide by sixty.

116
00:09:25.371 --> 00:09:28.415
Conservative, Typical and Optimistic choose different minutes.

117
00:09:28.415 --> 00:09:32.763
Monetary value appears only after you enter an hourly value.

118
00:09:32.763 --> 00:09:43.633
A fictional twelve tasks at thirty minutes each gives six expert-equivalent hours; at fifty dollars an hour that is three hundred dollars, not proven savings.

119
00:09:44.283 --> 00:09:49.063
Unknown categories remain in observed totals but may have no valuation.

120
00:09:49.063 --> 00:09:59.056
If any selected task row lacks credits, the aggregate credit and cost estimates are unavailable rather than pretending the missing credits are zero.

121
00:09:59.056 --> 00:10:01.663
Read the coverage notice and Basis.

122
00:10:01.663 --> 00:10:09.483
Task PAYG estimates do not allocate prepaid capacity or licences, and expert-equivalent work is not measured time saved.

123
00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:14.847
Optimization summarises reclaim, downsize, hold, monitor and upgrade recommendations.

124
00:10:14.847 --> 00:10:21.043
Right-size net combines modeled savings and upgrade costs at the pay-as-you-go rate.

125
00:10:21.043 --> 00:10:27.239
Reclaimable and dormant allowance are potential capacity changes, not guaranteed cash back.

126
00:10:27.239 --> 00:10:34.468
Savings matter only when released credits avoid a purchase that you would otherwise make.

127
00:10:34.468 --> 00:10:37.050
Nothing here changes permissions automatically.

128
00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:43.922
The action labels are built from the latest four weeks of credits and historical active-week persistence.

129
00:10:43.922 --> 00:10:49.367
Changing the displayed period can change the credit column without rebuilding those baseline labels.

130
00:10:49.367 --> 00:10:58.700
Recommended allowance adds fifteen percent headroom, rounds up to a two-hundred-and-fifty-credit step, and has a minimum of two hundred and fifty for positive use.

131
00:10:59.367 --> 00:11:07.549
Expand the queue and start with the largest net impact, but confirm leave, rollout timing and expected work with the owner.

132
00:11:07.549 --> 00:11:14.953
Persistent overuse means over the weekly equivalent of a monthly limit in at least sixty percent of active weeks.

133
00:11:14.953 --> 00:11:19.239
A temporary spike calls for a different action from sustained demand.

134
00:11:19.239 --> 00:11:21.967
Treat every recommendation as a review signal.

135
00:11:22.617 --> 00:11:27.303
Cowork Forecast shows a current cost basis and a twelve-month planning range.

136
00:11:27.303 --> 00:11:35.894
Its baseline is the selected period credits at today’s blended rate; it is not automatically normalised when you choose a longer period.

137
00:11:35.894 --> 00:11:39.799
For a monthly planning conversation, check the selected window first.

138
00:11:39.799 --> 00:11:45.267
The fitted trend uses the selected people and service across the available weekly history.

139
00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:57.375
The weekly trend uses a straight-line fit, translates relative weekly change to monthly growth, and bounds the fitted monthly rate between minus seventy-five and plus one hundred percent.

140
00:11:57.375 --> 00:12:01.455
Confidence also uses fit quality and a held-out recent-week backtest.

141
00:12:01.455 --> 00:12:08.800
The app requires six weeks for its growth range; with shorter history it leads with the current-rate result.

142
00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:12.523
The growth assumption can replace the fitted rate.

143
00:12:12.523 --> 00:12:17.518
Read the confidence and summary before treating a steep curve as a budget.

144
00:12:17.518 --> 00:12:22.128
A scenario range from flat to growth is not a confidence interval.

145
00:12:22.128 --> 00:12:27.890
The projection holds today’s blended rate rather than re-running future pack exhaustion month by month.

146
00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:32.500
Use it to compare planning cases, then validate the commercial implications separately.

147
00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:38.042
Studio Consumption combines daily tenant quantities with user and agent export snapshots.

148
00:12:38.042 --> 00:12:40.867
The period selector affects tenant totals only.

149
00:12:40.867 --> 00:12:43.288
User and agent detail is undated.

150
00:12:43.288 --> 00:12:49.340
Selecting the last seven days does not turn the user table into a seven-day table.

151
00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:56.200
Read the snapshot note, especially when the tenant total does not equal the sum of named users.

152
00:12:56.867 --> 00:13:00.274
The daily chart shows prepaid plus pay-as-you-go credits.

153
00:13:00.274 --> 00:13:08.367
Agent rankings in the user snapshot show the attributed portion, while model and feature breakdowns come from agent detail.

154
00:13:08.367 --> 00:13:12.200
Billing active users means distinct people in that snapshot.

155
00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:15.181
Average agents per user counts user-agent pairs.

156
00:13:15.181 --> 00:13:19.867
It is not a count of deployed agents in an inventory.

157
00:13:20.533 --> 00:13:26.458
Use the agent and user rankings to find a concentration worth discussing with an owner.

158
00:13:26.458 --> 00:13:30.803
Check whether a model or feature explains it, then use Cost.

159
00:13:30.803 --> 00:13:37.913
If user or agent files are missing, their detail is unavailable; a tenant export cannot invent person attribution.

160
00:13:37.913 --> 00:13:44.233
Do not describe a snapshot ranking as recent behaviour unless its export period is independently known.

161
00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:53.148
Studio Cost prices tenant prepaid quantities at the configured prepaid rate and pay-as-you-go quantities at the configured PAYG rate.

162
00:13:53.148 --> 00:13:57.463
Effective rate is total modeled cost divided by consumed credits.

163
00:13:57.463 --> 00:14:03.504
PAYG share is a share of credits, not necessarily the same percentage of cost.

164
00:14:03.504 --> 00:14:08.250
The daily chart lets you inspect when the billing mix changes.

165
00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:16.772
The tables labelled Estimated value by person and by agent use user-snapshot billable credits at the PAYG rate.

166
00:14:16.772 --> 00:14:24.594
Despite the word value, they are cost estimates of consumption, not productivity value or measured return on investment.

167
00:14:24.594 --> 00:14:28.505
Model and feature costs similarly price billed agent-snapshot credits.

168
00:14:28.505 --> 00:14:32.850
They are not an allocation of the selected-period tenant bill.

169
00:14:33.533 --> 00:14:42.114
If one agent dominates the cost estimate, inspect its billed quantity and model or feature mix, then confirm the appropriate rate.

170
00:14:42.114 --> 00:14:45.383
Keep daily tenant cost separate from snapshot attribution.

171
00:14:45.383 --> 00:14:49.878
A difference may reflect grain and coverage, not a billing defect.

172
00:14:49.878 --> 00:14:57.233
Use Optimization to prioritise review, and reconciliation only when period, currency and billing pool can actually be aligned.

173
00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:04.376
Studio Optimization highlights consumption without entitlement, unlicensed credit share, unused agents and concentration.

174
00:15:04.376 --> 00:15:15.746
The entitlement measure uses the latest date in the selected tenant scope, taking each environment’s entitlement once rather than adding repeated daily capacity.

175
00:15:15.746 --> 00:15:24.150
Unlicensed means no Microsoft 365 Copilot licence on user-snapshot rows; it does not prove access was unauthorised.

176
00:15:24.867 --> 00:15:32.467
An agent with at least a quarter of snapshot consumption and at least half billed is flagged for review.

177
00:15:32.467 --> 00:15:35.267
At least three quarters billed is investigate.

178
00:15:35.267 --> 00:15:38.067
High volume with better coverage is monitor.

179
00:15:38.067 --> 00:15:40.067
Zero agent-snapshot consumption is unused.

180
00:15:40.067 --> 00:15:42.067
Absorbed is the non-billed share.

181
00:15:42.067 --> 00:15:46.467
These labels describe billing patterns, not answer quality or safety assessments.

182
00:15:47.117 --> 00:15:53.129
Use the queue to select an agent, then look at its environment and heaviest users.

183
00:15:53.129 --> 00:15:57.138
Low use can mean a new or intentionally occasional agent.

184
00:15:57.138 --> 00:16:05.155
Days of prepaid capacity remaining uses current headroom and average draw, not a promise that capacity will last that long.

185
00:16:05.155 --> 00:16:10.767
Confirm entitlement configuration and future demand before retiring an agent or buying more capacity.

186
00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:18.579
Studio Forecast starts with tenant consumption divided by the calendar span, not just days with records.

187
00:16:18.579 --> 00:16:24.816
It scales that daily average to a thirty-day planning month at today’s effective rate.

188
00:16:24.816 --> 00:16:28.826
The chart compares a flat case with applied growth.

189
00:16:28.826 --> 00:16:33.281
Group allocations use user-snapshot shares; agent allocations use agent-snapshot shares.

190
00:16:33.281 --> 00:16:36.400
Neither is a measured future departmental bill.

191
00:16:37.117 --> 00:16:41.557
The fitted model uses a log-linear trend of trailing seven-day averages.

192
00:16:41.557 --> 00:16:45.997
It needs at least twenty-eight calendar days and seven positive-consumption days.

193
00:16:45.997 --> 00:16:49.629
Missing dates in the fit are treated as zero.

194
00:16:49.629 --> 00:16:54.473
Fitted monthly growth is bounded between minus thirty and plus thirty percent.

195
00:16:54.473 --> 00:16:59.317
With insufficient fit history, the model projects the observed daily average flat.

196
00:17:00.033 --> 00:17:06.696
The interface limits its growth presentation when there are fewer than fourteen days or too few observed dates.

197
00:17:06.696 --> 00:17:10.028
A longer calendar span can still have weak coverage.

198
00:17:10.028 --> 00:17:14.840
Read the summary and note rather than assuming a confidence label guarantees accuracy.

199
00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:22.983
Use an explicit scenario for a planned rollout, and explain that it is your assumption rather than a discovery in the data.

200
00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:27.990
GitHub Consumption is based on billed AI-credit usage in the export.

201
00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:34.620
Code completions and next-edit suggestions are not billed here, so they do not appear in these counts.

202
00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:38.520
Active developers means developers present in the selected usage records.

203
00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:45.150
A developer absent from this view may still be using capabilities that do not generate billed credits.

204
00:17:45.867 --> 00:17:48.261
The daily chart shows credit changes.

205
00:17:48.261 --> 00:17:51.454
Group rankings show attribution through the username mapping.

206
00:17:51.454 --> 00:17:56.243
Model bars show where credits go, with tiers classified in the data.

207
00:17:56.243 --> 00:18:05.023
Coding agent share and powerful model share are shares of credits, not shares of people or proof that a model was necessary.

208
00:18:05.023 --> 00:18:07.817
Use the filters to compare like-for-like work.

209
00:18:08.533 --> 00:18:13.062
Read the Basis and Concentration panels before acting on the heaviest-developer list.

210
00:18:13.062 --> 00:18:19.477
A small group can account for a large fraction of billed credits without representing all product use.

211
00:18:19.477 --> 00:18:24.382
Check whether the workload is appropriate, then use Cost for its financial effect.

212
00:18:24.382 --> 00:18:28.533
Do not turn low billed-credit use into a general productivity judgement.

213
00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:33.560
GitHub Cost separates monthly seat cost from the selected-period overage export.

214
00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:41.091
Gross value is before the included allowance; allowance absorbed is the export discount amount; overage is its net amount.

215
00:18:41.091 --> 00:18:47.036
The seat number remains a monthly cost even if you select several months of usage.

216
00:18:47.036 --> 00:18:51.000
Do not read their combination as a multi-month invoice total.

217
00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:57.000
Current gross, absorbed and net values come from the billing usage export.

218
00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:01.858
Changing the future credit rate does not rewrite those recorded amounts.

219
00:19:01.858 --> 00:19:08.483
Group and model tables identify cost concentration, while Chat versus coding agent separates usage types.

220
00:19:08.483 --> 00:19:15.550
The cost-per-developer card uses seats plus period overage over the licensed roster, not just active developers.

221
00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:22.547
For a current-month review, select the latest month and confirm how much of that month the export contains.

222
00:19:22.547 --> 00:19:27.132
A partial month should not be mistaken for a full monthly run rate.

223
00:19:27.132 --> 00:19:36.300
Use the daily cost chart to understand allowance absorption and overage, and compare with your actual billing agreement before changing licences or making a budget commitment.

224
00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:44.381
GitHub Optimization flags seats with no billed credit records in the selection and shows their seat cost at list price.

225
00:19:44.381 --> 00:19:49.211
That is a review queue, not proof of inactivity across the whole product.

226
00:19:49.211 --> 00:19:51.441
Unbilled completions are outside this dataset.

227
00:19:51.441 --> 00:19:58.500
A narrow model or usage filter can also affect the apparent gap, so clear filters for a roster-wide review.

228
00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:03.046
Pool used divides latest-month credits by today’s included pool.

229
00:20:03.046 --> 00:20:07.320
More than one hundred percent indicates credits beyond that pool.

230
00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:11.593
The top-ten-percent share and powerful-model share help prioritise a discussion.

231
00:20:11.593 --> 00:20:21.850
Developer rows and GitHub cost centres show different ways to locate demand; the seat plan table shows the corresponding roster and included monthly credits.

232
00:20:22.533 --> 00:20:30.914
Before reclaiming a seat, verify the roster is current, usernames map correctly and the person does not rely on unbilled features.

233
00:20:30.914 --> 00:20:34.905
Before downgrading a model, understand the task and quality requirements.

234
00:20:34.905 --> 00:20:38.098
These views support an informed review with owners.

235
00:20:38.098 --> 00:20:44.483
They do not measure code quality, useful output, or the causal effect of a model choice.

236
00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:48.726
GitHub Forecast starts from the latest month of credits.

237
00:20:48.726 --> 00:20:56.171
It projects credit demand, subtracts the included pool, prices positive overage at the configured rate, and adds monthly seats.

238
00:20:56.171 --> 00:21:02.832
When a future month ends on or after the configured change date, the new per-seat allowance applies.

239
00:21:02.832 --> 00:21:06.750
The pool can change even with a flat demand scenario.

240
00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:13.300
Observed monthly growth compares the first and last months across their elapsed month distance.

241
00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:18.732
The interface needs five months before presenting fitted growth as its planning range.

242
00:21:18.732 --> 00:21:22.075
With less history it emphasises the current-rate result.

243
00:21:22.075 --> 00:21:30.850
Partial first or latest months can distort the comparison, so check export completeness rather than relying only on the month count.

244
00:21:31.533 --> 00:21:37.038
Use Pool by plan and the allowance change date to explain a step in the curve.

245
00:21:37.038 --> 00:21:43.232
Confirm that date and future allowance against your agreement; the defaults are not a statement of current policy.

246
00:21:43.232 --> 00:21:46.328
Compare flat and growth cases, and document any override.

247
00:21:46.328 --> 00:21:51.833
Neither case is a confidence interval or a guarantee of the next twelve months of cost.

248
00:21:52.533 --> 00:22:00.625
Azure Spend includes models, AI services and supporting costs such as storage, networking and compute from the solution export.

249
00:22:00.625 --> 00:22:05.736
Choose Actual or Amortized basis, a subscription, application, service and one currency.

250
00:22:05.736 --> 00:22:11.273
Different currencies must not be added as though they were the same money.

251
00:22:11.273 --> 00:22:16.383
The composition chart shows how the selected cost is distributed over time.

252
00:22:17.033 --> 00:22:21.106
Model share divides model cost by selected whole-solution cost.

253
00:22:21.106 --> 00:22:23.368
Supporting cost is shown separately.

254
00:22:23.368 --> 00:22:31.513
Tagged allocation is the share of cost carrying a department tag, not proof that the tag is correct.

255
00:22:31.513 --> 00:22:36.491
Tokens, speech hours, document pages and images are different workload measures.

256
00:22:36.491 --> 00:22:44.183
Cost per million PAYG tokens uses PAYG token cost and PAYG tokens, not the entire Azure bill.

257
00:22:44.867 --> 00:22:51.056
Sort the resource table, inspect the application and service, and look for untagged cost.

258
00:22:51.056 --> 00:22:56.804
This view uses the loaded solution export rather than the Overview date selector.

259
00:22:56.804 --> 00:23:01.225
Match its export period before comparing it with another product.

260
00:23:01.225 --> 00:23:10.067
Use Capacity and health for deployment context and Reconciliation for an aligned shared-pool comparison, not for summing unrelated support costs.

261
00:23:10.783 --> 00:23:16.657
Capacity and health shows deployment inventory, provisioned throughput units, utilisation and request-health indicators.

262
00:23:16.657 --> 00:23:24.789
Capacity comes from the latest metric date and latest snapshot per deployment, not a sum of daily capacity.

263
00:23:24.789 --> 00:23:31.565
Requests and health values belong to the selected snapshot records and their exported measurement period.

264
00:23:31.565 --> 00:23:36.083
This is a stored export, not a live service monitor.

265
00:23:36.783 --> 00:23:40.231
PTU utilisation is weighted by provisioned capacity.

266
00:23:40.231 --> 00:23:45.647
Request-based error and throttle rates divide eligible error counts by requests.

267
00:23:45.647 --> 00:23:47.617
Latency is request weighted.

268
00:23:47.617 --> 00:23:53.034
A deployment below thirty percent PTU utilisation is a review signal.

269
00:23:53.034 --> 00:24:02.883
Do not average displayed percentages without accounting for those denominators, and do not confuse 429 throttles with 5xx server errors.

270
00:24:03.533 --> 00:24:09.906
Filter by subscription, application or SKU, then inspect the low-utilisation and latency rankings.

271
00:24:09.906 --> 00:24:14.317
Check peaks, response-time needs and expected demand before resizing.

272
00:24:14.317 --> 00:24:23.140
Low mean utilisation does not guarantee recoverable savings; provisioned capacity can exist to meet burst or latency requirements.

273
00:24:23.140 --> 00:24:30.983
A health issue also needs its measurement window and operational context before you attribute a cause.

274
00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:37.686
Reconciliation compares a product-derived usage estimate with Azure actuals for a shared billing pool.

275
00:24:37.686 --> 00:24:40.679
Choose a billing period, currency and pool.

276
00:24:40.679 --> 00:24:49.231
The app requires matching start and end dates, one pool, one valid currency, and both representations before calculating a difference.

277
00:24:49.231 --> 00:24:56.500
All periods or all pools can deliberately make the comparison unavailable rather than producing a misleading variance.

278
00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:02.753
Positive difference means Azure actual is above the estimate; negative means below.

279
00:25:02.753 --> 00:25:10.619
Evidence is explicitly fictional sample evidence in this demo, not a verified explanation of a customer bill.

280
00:25:10.619 --> 00:25:12.933
Residual is difference minus evidence.

281
00:25:12.933 --> 00:25:22.650
Detail rows represent separate billing forms, so an individual row may not itself contain both sides needed for a comparable difference.

282
00:25:23.367 --> 00:25:30.567
For a fictional estimate of one thousand dollars and actual of eleven hundred, the difference is one hundred.

283
00:25:30.567 --> 00:25:34.567
If fictional evidence is sixty, the unexplained residual is forty.

284
00:25:34.567 --> 00:25:37.767
Do not claim that arithmetic proves the cause.

285
00:25:37.767 --> 00:25:41.367
Supporting Azure solution costs are outside this shared pool.

286
00:25:41.367 --> 00:25:45.367
Investigate aligned source evidence before labelling any real amount explained.

287
00:25:46.033 --> 00:25:49.304
Guide gathers the written explanation beside the data views.

288
00:25:49.304 --> 00:25:52.574
Start here offers product cards and a reading route.

289
00:25:52.574 --> 00:25:58.389
It also explains the app layout and how charts were reshaped from the Power BI report.

290
00:25:58.389 --> 00:26:06.383
Ranked bars may replace a donut or treemap; the important question remains the same: what is being counted and on which basis?

291
00:26:07.033 --> 00:26:10.539
Cowork explains person credit allowances and policy allocation.

292
00:26:10.539 --> 00:26:13.606
Studio explains tenant consumption and snapshot attribution.

293
00:26:13.606 --> 00:26:17.988
GitHub separates billed credit demand from seats and included pools.

294
00:26:17.988 --> 00:26:21.055
Azure adds whole-solution spend and deployment context.

295
00:26:21.055 --> 00:26:31.133
You can start with one product, but you should not assume the other sources are present just because the navigation offers their pages.

296
00:26:31.783 --> 00:26:36.300
Confirm source coverage, read the denominator, then decide what to investigate.

297
00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:42.458
The synthetic sample makes the interface useful for learning, not for estimating your organisation’s consumption.

298
00:26:42.458 --> 00:26:46.564
The explainer is a secondary link from the app header.

299
00:26:46.564 --> 00:26:53.133
Use the written Guide when you need to revisit a definition without replaying the full video.

300
00:26:53.783 --> 00:26:59.578
How to read explains the boundary between exported quantities, identity enrichment and modeled cost.

301
00:26:59.578 --> 00:27:03.303
A source may record credits without recording a person.

302
00:27:03.303 --> 00:27:06.614
A department breakdown depends on a valid join.

303
00:27:06.614 --> 00:27:10.753
A rate assumption can price activity but cannot prove value.

304
00:27:10.753 --> 00:27:16.133
Keep those distinctions visible when you share a screenshot or report a headline.

305
00:27:16.783 --> 00:27:20.187
Optimization labels are rules over the available records.

306
00:27:20.187 --> 00:27:21.889
Forecasts are planning cases.

307
00:27:21.889 --> 00:27:24.868
Expert-equivalent task work is an assumption-based estimate.

308
00:27:24.868 --> 00:27:32.952
None of these is a causal measurement of productivity, a guarantee of savings or an instruction to remove access.

309
00:27:32.952 --> 00:27:38.483
Read the caveats on the page before taking an administrative or purchasing action.

310
00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:44.917
When you communicate a result, state the product, dates, source completeness and rate basis.

311
00:27:44.917 --> 00:27:51.858
Say whether it is a daily tenant total, weekly person record, monthly billable quantity or undated snapshot.

312
00:27:51.858 --> 00:28:01.250
If those details cannot be established, report the limitation rather than turning a plausible-looking chart into a stronger claim than the data supports.

313
00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:09.977
Use the Cowork product notes to review how weekly credits meet person limits, policies and the task export.

314
00:28:09.977 --> 00:28:14.882
Person allowance, policy plan allowance and prepaid capacity are different controls.

315
00:28:14.882 --> 00:28:23.800
The notes complement the Consumption, Cost, Chargeback, Tasks, Optimization and Forecast views; they are not another set of usage records.

316
00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:28.937
Check a four-week baseline when interpreting fixed optimization recommendations.

317
00:28:28.937 --> 00:28:32.926
Check selected task coverage separately from credit coverage.

318
00:28:32.926 --> 00:28:41.900
A cost allocation needs spending-policy billing fields, and a task value estimate needs explicit minute and hourly assumptions.

319
00:28:42.617 --> 00:28:46.969
Use Open Cowork & Work IQ to return to the product tab.

320
00:28:46.969 --> 00:28:50.234
Start with one question and the smallest useful selection.

321
00:28:50.234 --> 00:28:56.038
If the required export is missing, collect it through your approved process and reload the app.

322
00:28:56.038 --> 00:29:02.567
The guide explains interpretation; it does not connect to services or fill a missing source with fabricated results.

323
00:29:03.283 --> 00:29:11.559
Use the Studio notes to revisit the distinction between daily tenant totals and undated user or agent snapshots.

324
00:29:11.559 --> 00:29:14.778
The tenant source establishes consumption over dates.

325
00:29:14.778 --> 00:29:16.617
User records support attribution.

326
00:29:16.617 --> 00:29:20.295
Agent records support billing-pattern, model and feature analysis.

327
00:29:20.295 --> 00:29:24.433
Those sources can cover different populations or reporting windows.

328
00:29:25.117 --> 00:29:28.920
A tenant date filter does not date a snapshot.

329
00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:35.260
A person cost estimate at PAYG is not an allocation of the selected-period tenant invoice.

330
00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:42.867
Where attribution is absent, keep the gap visible rather than forcing totals to reconcile by distributing it arbitrarily.

331
00:29:43.533 --> 00:29:47.345
Use Open Copilot Studio to return to the product tab.

332
00:29:47.345 --> 00:29:50.775
Start with one question and the smallest useful selection.

333
00:29:50.775 --> 00:29:56.873
If the required export is missing, collect it through your approved process and reload the app.

334
00:29:56.873 --> 00:30:03.733
The guide explains interpretation; it does not connect to services or fill a missing source with fabricated results.

335
00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:12.879
Use the GitHub notes to connect billed AI credits, the seat roster, username mapping and included monthly pool.

336
00:30:12.879 --> 00:30:16.157
Gross, discount and net are export measures.

337
00:30:16.157 --> 00:30:20.372
Seat prices and future included-credit settings are planning assumptions.

338
00:30:20.372 --> 00:30:23.650
Unbilled completions are outside the billed-credit view.

339
00:30:24.367 --> 00:30:27.602
No billed credits is not no product use.

340
00:30:27.602 --> 00:30:32.050
Compare a monthly seat amount with a clearly stated usage period.

341
00:30:32.050 --> 00:30:38.117
Confirm future allowance dates before treating a forecast cost step as a genuine commercial change.

342
00:30:38.783 --> 00:30:42.481
Use Open GitHub Copilot to return to the product tab.

343
00:30:42.481 --> 00:30:45.810
Start with one question and the smallest useful selection.

344
00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:51.727
If the required export is missing, collect it through your approved process and reload the app.

345
00:30:51.727 --> 00:30:58.383
The guide explains interpretation; it does not connect to services or fill a missing source with fabricated results.

346
00:30:59.033 --> 00:31:06.938
Use the Azure notes to distinguish model-only export costs from the whole solution, including supporting services.

347
00:31:06.938 --> 00:31:10.890
Health combines inventory snapshots with exported request metrics.

348
00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:14.843
Reconciliation compares representations only when scope is aligned.

349
00:31:14.843 --> 00:31:19.783
These are different views of the solution, not interchangeable totals.

350
00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:23.140
Keep currencies and cost bases separate.

351
00:31:23.140 --> 00:31:33.900
Do not add capacity snapshots over dates, infer guaranteed savings from low utilisation, or pass fictional reconciliation evidence off as a real billing explanation.

352
00:31:34.617 --> 00:31:38.742
Use Open Azure AI Foundry to return to the product tab.

353
00:31:38.742 --> 00:31:42.117
Start with one question and the smallest useful selection.

354
00:31:42.117 --> 00:31:48.117
If the required export is missing, collect it through your approved process and reload the app.

355
00:31:48.117 --> 00:31:54.867
The guide explains interpretation; it does not connect to services or fill a missing source with fabricated results.

356
00:31:55.533 --> 00:31:58.621
The searchable Glossary is the final Guide view.

357
00:31:58.621 --> 00:32:04.411
Search a term such as allowance, billed, utilisation or forecast when a label is unclear.

358
00:32:04.411 --> 00:32:10.200
The same familiar word can refer to a different source or denominator in another product.

359
00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:17.533
Use the definition together with the page’s help text and visible basis, not as permission to combine unlike units.

360
00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:23.400
A screenshot of a KPI can hide its period selector and source note.

361
00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:28.200
If you share a result, include those details in the accompanying explanation.

362
00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:31.000
For a modeled estimate, include the assumption.

363
00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:34.200
For a missing source, state what is missing.

364
00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:39.800
For an undated snapshot, say that explicitly rather than calling it this week’s activity.

365
00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:48.262
Use the glossary to make a review conversation precise: which credits, which period, which price, which owner and which decision?

366
00:32:48.262 --> 00:32:53.731
If the evidence is not sufficient, your next action is to improve the evidence.

367
00:32:53.731 --> 00:32:59.200
That is more useful than an apparently precise recommendation built on an unknown denominator.

368
00:32:59.867 --> 00:33:02.840
Begin on Overview and confirm coverage.

369
00:33:02.840 --> 00:33:09.282
Choose the largest relevant driver, then use that product’s Consumption and Cost views.

370
00:33:09.282 --> 00:33:11.760
Review optimization signals with owners.

371
00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:15.724
Treat forecasts as scenarios and check their history.

372
00:33:15.724 --> 00:33:20.679
For Azure, inspect whole-solution spend, capacity and aligned reconciliation separately.

373
00:33:20.679 --> 00:33:27.617
That keeps the investigation connected without pretending all four products share one billing model.

374
00:33:28.283 --> 00:33:42.467
Remember the main caveats: weekly Cowork windows versus monthly limits, Studio snapshots versus daily totals, billed GitHub credits versus all product activity, and Azure snapshot capacity versus period health.

375
00:33:42.467 --> 00:33:46.380
Task work equivalent is estimated, not measured savings.

376
00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:50.292
Fictional reconciliation evidence is not a live explanation.

377
00:33:50.292 --> 00:33:55.183
A missing source stays a limitation, not an invented number.

378
00:33:55.867 --> 00:33:59.531
This recording used synthetic sample data and AI-generated narration.

379
00:33:59.531 --> 00:34:03.195
No customer data or live tenant telemetry was used.

380
00:34:03.195 --> 00:34:11.745
Your own next step is to confirm a source and an owner, test one explanation, and make the smallest justified decision.

381
00:34:11.745 --> 00:34:18.667
Consumption Central is most useful when every number arrives with its basis, not just a larger claim.
